HOW TO INSTALL ARDUINO IDE WITH NECESSARY COMPONENTS:
1. install arduino IDE

2. Go to File > Preferences (Arduino > Settings on macOS). 
   In "Additional boards manager URLs", paste: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/espressif/arduino-esp32/gh-pages/package_esp32_index.json — then click OK.

3. Open Tools > Board > Boards Manager, search "esp32", and install the package by Espressif Systems. 
   Make sure the version installed starts with 3.x (not the older 2.x line) — the version number shows right in the Boards Manager list before you hit Install.

4. Go to Tools > Board > esp32 > ESP32C6 Dev Module (exact name may vary slightly, e.g. "ESP32C6 Dev Module"). Then plug in your board and pick the right port under Tools > Port.

5. Open Sketch > Include Library > Manage Libraries, search "VL53L0X", and install the one with Author/Maintainer listed as Pololu (there are a few VL53L0X libraries from other maintainers
   Make sure you grab Pololu's, since that's the one the organisers likely built their examples around).

6. In the same Library Manager, search "MPU6050". Common choices are "MPU6050" by Electronic Cats or "Adafruit MPU6050" 
   if the organisers haven't specified which, either works fine for basic accelerometer/gyro reading, 
   but note the API calls differ slightly between them, so pick one and stick with it once you see which example code style the organisers use.

7. Restart the IDE, then check File > Examples — you should see VL53L0X and MPU6050 example sketches listed near the bottom under "Examples from Custom Libraries". 
   Open one, select your ESP32-C6 board/port, and try a compile (Verify, not Upload) to confirm everything's linked up correctly before the actual event.

One thing worth flagging: since the organisers haven't specified which MPU6050 library, 
if their video series or docs later say to use a specific one (Electronic Cats vs. Adafruit vs. i2cdevlib), 
it's worth swapping to match — the function names and calibration steps differ enough that mismatched example code won't just drop in. 
Worth checking the organisers' site again closer to the event date in case the promised docs go up.
